WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.630 --> 00:00:09.440
Another Regeneration milestone was celebrated
recently, as large crowds gathered for the

00:00:09.440 --> 00:00:12.840
official opening of the Kaiapoi Riverview
Terraces and boardwalk

00:00:12.840 --> 00:00:15.360
with Mayor David Ayers cutting the ribbon.

00:00:17.020 --> 00:00:20.980
It’s actually exemplifying what we’re
trying to do through the Regeneration programme,

00:00:20.980 --> 00:00:24.080
not simply repair what was broken during the
earthquakes,

00:00:24.080 --> 00:00:27.420
but actually take Kaiapoi another step forward.

00:00:28.840 --> 00:00:31.620
The opening was timed to coincide with the
return of the

00:00:31.620 --> 00:00:33.740
Kaiapoi River Carnival and Boat Show.

00:00:33.740 --> 00:00:37.120
We haven’t had a River Carnival since around
about the 90s,

00:00:37.120 --> 00:00:39.780
and it’s really good to see activity on the river again.

00:00:39.780 --> 00:00:43.620
And of course, that’s what this redevelopment
of this part of Kaiapoi is all about,

00:00:43.620 --> 00:00:45.540
in getting people back onto the river.

00:00:45.900 --> 00:00:50.280
A huge amount of work was completed in recent
months to get the terraces ready.

00:00:50.280 --> 00:00:54.040
Wooden decking was laid, a competition-winning
poem was installed

00:00:54.040 --> 00:00:57.520
and Māori design elements were sandblasted into the steps.

00:00:57.960 --> 00:01:02.180
Down by the new coastguard building, dredging
was completed to allow for the installation

00:01:02.180 --> 00:01:03.940
of the first pontoon.

00:01:03.940 --> 00:01:08.200
A second, larger pontoon will be installed
between the Riverview terraces and wharf

00:01:08.200 --> 00:01:09.760
later this year.

00:01:09.760 --> 00:01:13.060
And with the adjacent commercial development
now also open,

00:01:13.060 --> 00:01:17.120
Kaiapoi’s Marine Precinct is starting to take on a life of its own.

